  "updated": "2026-08-05T20:04:20.069136+00:00",
  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nThe high pressure system which brought nice, fair weather to the\narea over the past couple of days is slowly losing influence \nover us as it drift eastward today. A weak and broad low \npressure system has moved up from the southeastern U.S. into the\nMid- Appalachian and Mid-Atlantic region today. We are on the \nnorthwest fringes of this weather system. It has brought back \nsome moisture into our atmosphere, especially over eastern Ohio \nand northwester Pennsylvania. We currently have scattered \nshowers and isolated thunderstorms over portions of east central\nand northeast Ohio as well as NWPA this afternoon, generally \nalong and east of the I-77 corridor. The rest of the area will \nremain rainfree and partly cloudy for the remainder of this \nafternoon and evening. The scattered convection over NEOH and \nNWPA this afternoon will gradually decrease in coverage and move\neastward out of the area this evening. \n\nThe next weather system to impact the area will be a weak and \nslow moving cold front that is currently located from the \ncentral Great Lakes region down into the Cornbelt Belt of Iowa. \nThere will also be a change in our weather pattern with a broad \nupper level trough slowly shifting from the north central U.S \neastward into the Great Lakes region later this week into the \nweekend. We will be near the base of this broad upper level \ntrough for several days starting Thursday through the weekend. \nWe will be watching for little mid-level disturbances or weak \nshort-wave troughs to round the base of the broader upper level \ntrough to bring us multiple rounds of scattered showers and \nthunderstorms through the weekend. This weather pattern setup \nlooks increasingly likely it will continue to be somewhat \nunsettled going into early next week as well. \n\nThe weak cold front will move closer to our area from the west\ntomorrow and eventually stall out over the area or just \nnorthwest of us. This frontal boundary will help in pooling of \nmoisture along and ahead of it over the next several days with \nPWAT values up to 1.8 inch at times. We will have scattered POPs\nacross much of the area Thursday, especially during the \nafternoon and evening. Most of the convection will be diurnally \ndriven with the daytime heating. There will be a slightly better\nmid-level disturbance to interact with the frontal boundary on \nFriday which will bring higher rain chances. Some of that \nconvection on Friday may linger into Friday night with likely \nPOPs. Given the higher moisture content that is expected to be \naround, localized heavy rainfall may be possible with any slow \nmoving and training of storms. We will keep the higher POPs in \nthe forecast through the weekend. Saturday afternoon/evening and\nSunday evening/overnight will be the two time frames that we \nwill have the highest rain chances for showers and storms. It \nappears that the stalled frontal boundary will stick around and \nbring unsettled weather Monday and perhaps into Tuesday with \nhigher POPs in the forecast. We may finally see a drier forecast\nreturn by the middle of next week as the front gets a push to \nmove out of our area. It is very possible that we have several \ninches of rainfall across the area this weekend into early next \nweek.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nTemperatures are expected to be near or at just slightly above \naverage for early August standards through the weekend into \nearly next week. High temperatures will generally be in the\nlower to middle 80s Thursday through Monday with overnight low\ntemps in the 60s. We may see temperatures drop a few degrees by\nthe middle of next week when a cold front pushes through the \narea.",
